  "answer": "The public help article implies precise page/paragraph-level PDF citations, but the critical nuance is that this capability applies only to natively digital, text-searchable PDFs. Scanned, photocopied, or image-only PDFs lack a native text layer and must run through OCR, which is a heuristic process [NL-002#NL-002-S09]. Low-quality scans (blurry, misaligned, multi-column, or containing handwriting) can produce broken or incomplete text blocks, leading to missing citations, incorrect page links, or TutorChat stating it cannot find the answer even when it exists on the page [NL-002#NL-002-S09][NL-006#NL-006-S03]. In practice, this means citation anchors may link to page one of a long document instead of the specific page, or no citation link is generated at all [NL-011#NL-011-S05][NL-018#NL-018-S04]. The help article itself acknowledges this limitation, noting that low-quality scanned PDFs may struggle to generate page-specific citations due to OCR limitations [NL-028#NL-028-S03]. Therefore, the nuance to include is: while PDFs generally support citations, scanned or image-only PDFs require high-quality OCR; otherwise, citations may be missing, broken, or imprecise (e.g., wrong page or section location), and customers are advised to convert scanned documents to digital text-searchable formats before upload [NL-002#NL-002-S09][NL-006#NL-006-S04][NL-028#NL-028-S03].\n\nANSWER_STATUS: answer\nUSED_MEMORY_CLAIMS: none",
